                "The Capital Conservation Buffer (CCB) establishes an additional 2.5 percent CET1 capital requirement above the 4.5 percent minimum, elevating the total baseline CET1 requirement to 7.0 percent (and Total Capital to 10.5 percent globally, or 11.5 percent in India). The primary regulatory philosophy of the CCB is to ensure that banks accumulate substantial equity cushions during normal economic times that can be drawn down safely as losses materialize during periods of economic distress. Unlike minimum capital requirements—which trigger formal regulatory sanctions or prompt corrective action if breached—breaching the CCB does not force bank liquidation. Instead, it triggers automatic, legally binding restrictions on discretionary capital distributions.",
                "Under the Maximum Distributable Amount (MDA) framework, if a bank's CET1 ratio dips into the 2.5 percent CCB band, statutory payout caps are enforced across four quartiles: (1) Top Quartile (CET1 CCB between 1.875% and 2.50%): Bank can distribute up to 60 percent of current-year earnings; (2) Second Quartile (CET1 CCB between 1.25% and 1.875%): Payout capped at 40 percent; (3) Third Quartile (CET1 CCB between 0.625% and 1.25%): Payout capped at 20 percent; and (4) Bottom Quartile (CET1 CCB < 0.625%): Payout capped at 0 percent. Restricted distributions encompass cash dividend payments, common share buybacks, and discretionary executive incentive bonuses, forcing the bank to retain all operating earnings to rebuild equity buffers.",
